`toCamel` only capitalized the letter *after* a separator and left the rest of the word untouched:

```
toCamel('LINE_TOTAL')  //  was → 'LINETOTAL'
```

It handled `snake_case` and `kebab-case` but silently mis-folded the legacy `SCREAMING_SNAKE` form the CSV export uses — which, because it's the boundary all four endpoints share, meant any SCREAMING key could leak a storage-style name to a client. The suite's one failing assertion was exactly this (`'LINETOTAL'` vs expected `'lineTotal'`).

## The fix (`src/wire.js`)
`toCamel` now detects the all-uppercase case and folds each word down before joining:

```js
export function toCamel(key) {
  const s = String(key)
  const parts = s.split(/[-_\s]+/).filter(Boolean)
  // SCREAMING_SNAKE / SCREAMING-KEBAB: fold words down, then camelCase.
  if (parts.length && parts.every((w) => w === w.toUpperCase() && /[A-Z]/.test(w))) {
    return parts
      .map((w, i) => (i === 0 ? w.toLowerCase() : w[0] + w.slice(1).toLowerCase()))
      .join('')
  }
  // snake_case / kebab-case / already camelCase.
  return s.replace(/[-_\s]+([a-zA-Z0-9])/g, (whole, char) => char.toUpperCase())
}
```

This keeps `line_total` → `lineTotal`, `line-total` → `lineTotal`, `id` → `id`, and now `LINE_TOTAL` → `lineTotal`, so every endpoint's payload — top-level and nested, including the round-trip back through `fromWire` — lands on the one camelCase convention. Fixing it at the boundary (not per-handler) is what keeps the nested shapes consistent across the four modules.

Note: I verified by running the file directly (`node test/wire.test.js`) because the `node --test` runner spawns per-file child processes, which the host sandbox blocks with `EPERM` — that's an environment limit, not a test failure.

